      "latency_ms": 2290.9,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 576,
      "output": "At its simplest, **water** is the most abundant and essential molecule for life on Earth. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, looking at it through chemical, physical, and ecological lenses:\n\n### 1. The Chemical Definition\nChemically, water is a compound made of two types of atoms: **Hydrogen** and **Oxygen**. Its famous chemical formula is **$H_2O$**. This means every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\n### 2. Physical Properties\nWater is unique because it is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states:\n*   **Liquid:** The water we drink, swim in, and rain from the sky.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Frozen water, found in glaciers, snow, and ice cubes.\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Invisible water vapor in the air, clouds, and steam.\n\nWater also has remarkable physical properties:\n*   **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is why it can carry nutrients through our bloodstreams and minerals through the soil.\n*   **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some insects to walk on water) and to other surfaces (adhesion—which helps water move up the roots of tall trees).\n*   **Density Anomaly:** Most substances shrink and become denser when they freeze, but water actually *expands* and becomes less dense. That is why ice floats on water. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, destroying aquatic life.\n\n### 3. The Earthly Context\n*   **Global Coverage:** About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered by water, mostly in oceans and seas (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water).\n*   **Scarcity:** Despite the Earth looking blue and wet, **only about 2.5% of Earth's water is freshwater**, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% is easily accessible for human use.\n\n### 4. Biological Importance\nWater is the cradle of life. \n*   The human body is made of about **60% water**.\n*   Cells in all living organisms (plants, animals, bacteria) are filled with water-based fluid, where all the metabolic processes of life take place.\n*   Without water, biological life as we know it cannot survive for more than a few days. \n\nIn short, water is a simple chemical molecule that acts as the lifeblood of our planet."
